  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Warm and dry today with sunny skies by this afternoon; breezy\n   easterly winds developing into tonight.\n\n-  Shower and thunderstorms Thursday into Friday, generally south\n   of Highway 12. Storms will be capable of brief heavy rain and \n   small hail.\n \n-  Another warming and drying trend Sunday through early next\n   week.\n\nShort lived high pressure over the region today will feature \npleasant, sunny, and near-seasonable weather, with high \ntemperatures reaching the 70s in western Montana and the 80s in \nIdaho. A pressure gradient will generate northeast winds of 10 to \n20 mph during the day. Tonight, this gradient will tighten, \nincreasing the potential for easterly wind gusts exceeding 20 to \n25 mph, particularly in channeled terrain along and west of the \nContinental Divide.\n\nA low-pressure system will approach southwest Idaho on Thursday, \ndrawing a surge of abundant monsoon moisture into the area. \nCombined with strong wind shear, this system will trigger \nthunderstorms capable of producing heavy rain, lightning, gusty \nwinds, and small hail. On Friday, potential afternoon heating \ncould increase atmospheric instability, elevating the threat for \nstronger storms capable of producing larger hail. The highest \nprobabilities for these thunderstorms will focus across central \nIdaho and west-central to southwest Montana, while northwest \nMontana will experience much lower chances for precipitation.\n\nAs the low-pressure system exits the region on Saturday, the focus \nfor showers and thunderstorms will shift east of the US-93 \ncorridor into central Montana, leaving the lower valleys of Idaho \nmostly dry with moderating temperatures. High pressure will then \nbuild across the region from Sunday into early next week. Expect \npatchy morning fog in river valleys followed by afternoon highs \npotentially reaching the 80s in Missoula and the lower valleys of \nIdaho. By mid-week, there is a 40 percent chance that a passing \nfrontal system could bring breezy conditions and temper the early-\nweek warmth.",
